Reply @KoBeWi :
> There is ResourceLoader::load_threaded_request(). It might still cause stutters, but they are rare and very minor (although I never tried it with 3D scenes).

I tried it, it does not make a difference, instead it makes this thumbnailing process unstable and outputs a lot of errors, so I switched back to `ResourceLoader::load()`. But it was few months ago, and I recalled that `ResourceLoader::load_threaded_request()` has some bug waiting to be fixed at that time. 

I said "multi-threaded scene loading still isn't possible now", what I really mean is the whole loading and instantiating process. It is not possible to process a scene without stuttering the main thread for now, any scene file has to load and instantiate in a blocking manner.

> The main problem I see is the usage of Main::iteration(). I know we already use it much in the editor, but it would be nice to avoid it if possible xd If not feasible then it's probably fine.

Not a problem anymore, as the scene preview now only runs on a thread, we can remove `Main::iteration()` and replace with a simple timeout. It was there to prevent the editor from freezing when running on main thread. 

The worst case here is timeout has reached and the preview viewport is blank, returns a blank or broken thumbnail image:

```c++
void EditorPackedScenePreviewPlugin::_wait_frame() const {
	const uint64_t max_wait_msecs = 3000; 
	const uint64_t prev_msec = OS::get_singleton()->get_ticks_msec();
	const uint64_t prev_frame = Engine::get_singleton()->get_frames_drawn();

	while (Engine::get_singleton()->get_frames_drawn() - prev_frame < 1) {
		// So we don't get stuck in here forever if something goes wrong
		if (OS::get_singleton()->get_ticks_msec() - prev_msec > max_wait_msecs) {
			break;
		}
		if (aborted.is_set()) {
			break;
		}
	}
}
```

Side note I already experienced several `Main::iteration()` nasty bugs when coding this PR, there are few more `Main::iteration` across the source code, from my experience many bugs are initiated via idle_frame callbacks, where it expects only to be emitted once per idle time. Just to let others know.

